      "source": "https://dover-foxcrofthistoricalsociety.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1922, Dover and Foxcroft merged across the Piscataquis River into a single town, uniting two communities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover-Foxcroft%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Dover‑Foxcroft sits near Maine’s geographic center, making it a natural crossroads of the state’s interior.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover-Foxcroft%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Piscataquis River powered early mills, including sawmills, a shingle mill, and a tannery, shaping the town’s industrial roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover-Foxcroft%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every late June, Dover‑Foxcroft hosts the Maine Whoopie Pie Festival, a one‑day celebration that drew 7,500 people in 2014.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover-Foxcroft%2C_Maine"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The American Woolen Company Foxcroft Mill, a historic district added to the National Register in 2012, showcases buildings from 1841 to 1944.",
